X	application-support	p	**No, not as a whole.** Application Support is where apps keep their own data: libraries, projects, chat history, downloaded content and settings too big for a preferences file. Deleting an app’s folder resets that app and can lose what you made in it.
X	application-support	p	**What you can remove:** the folders of apps you’ve already deleted. Moving an app to the Trash leaves its folder here, and once the app is gone for good, that folder is a leftover.
X	application-support	p	**Does it come back?** Only as an empty folder. An app creates a fresh one the next time it runs, but the old contents are gone.
X	application-support	h	Measure it
X	application-support	code	du -sh ~/Library/'Application Support'/* 2>/dev/null | sort -h | tail -15
X	application-support	h	How to clear it
X	application-support	li	1. Look for folders whose app you no longer have. Match each name to an app: `com.spotify.client` is Spotify, and a plain name is usually the app’s own.
X	application-support	li	2. Check the app really is gone, from Applications and from `~/Applications`, and that nothing else from the same maker uses the folder. The `Google` folder, for example, holds data for Chrome and Google’s other apps.
X	application-support	li	3. If there might be something of yours inside, such as exports or saved games, copy it out first.
X	application-support	li	4. Move the folder to the Trash, and empty the Trash once you’re sure.
X	application-support	p	For apps you still use, don’t delete their folder to save space. Remove downloads, old projects or offline content in the app’s own settings instead. To remove an app along with what it keeps, use its uninstaller if it has one: Apple calls that the best way to remove an app and its related data.
X	application-support	h	Sources
X	application-support	src	Apple: macOS Library Directory Details (Application Support): https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/FileManagement/Conceptual/FileSystemProgrammingGuide/MacOSXDirectories/MacOSXDirectories.html
X	application-support	src	Apple: Delete or uninstall apps on Mac: https://support.apple.com/en-us/102610
X	diagnostic-reports	p	**Yes.** Every file in it is a report macOS wrote after something crashed, hung or ran into trouble. Once you’ve read it, or sent it to whoever needs it, nothing on your Mac uses it.
X	diagnostic-reports	p	**What it costs you:** evidence. If an app keeps crashing and its developer asks for a report, a recent one helps, so keep those until the problem is fixed.
X	diagnostic-reports	p	**Does it come back?** Only when something fails again. A folder that refills quickly is a symptom worth fixing, not just clearing.
X	diagnostic-reports	h	Measure it
X	diagnostic-reports	code	du -sh ~/Library/Logs/DiagnosticReports
X	diagnostic-reports	code	ls -lt ~/Library/Logs/DiagnosticReports | head -20
X	diagnostic-reports	h	How to clear it
X	diagnostic-reports	li	1. Open Console and choose Crash Reports, or another kind of report.
X	diagnostic-reports	li	2. Select the reports you don’t need and choose File › Move to Trash.
X	diagnostic-reports	li	3. Empty the Trash.
X	diagnostic-reports	p	Your own reports can also go from Finder, in `~/Library/Logs/DiagnosticReports`. The ones in `/Library/Logs/DiagnosticReports` belong to the system: remove those through Console as an administrator rather than from Terminal.
X	diagnostic-reports	p	If an app keeps crashing, update it, and send one of its reports to the developer before deleting the rest. Developers don’t automatically see your crashes: for App Store apps, Apple passes reports on only from people who share analytics with app developers, a setting in System Settings › Privacy & Security › Analytics & Improvements.
X	diagnostic-reports	h	Sources
X	diagnostic-reports	src	Apple: View reports in Console on Mac: https://support.apple.com/guide/console/cnsl664be99a/mac
X	diagnostic-reports	src	Apple Developer: Acquiring crash reports and diagnostic logs: https://developer.apple.com/documentation/xcode/acquiring-crash-reports-and-diagnostic-logs
X	document-revisions	p	**Leave the folder itself.** It’s the database of past versions macOS keeps for documents you edit in apps that save automatically, such as Pages, Numbers, Keynote, TextEdit and Preview. Deleting it destroys every saved version at once, and nothing inside can be removed piece by piece by hand.
X	document-revisions	p	**What you can do:** delete versions you don’t need from inside the app with File › Revert To, or drop every version of one big document by duplicating it.
X	document-revisions	p	**Does it come back?** Yes: macOS keeps saving versions as you work, every hour and whenever you save, rename or duplicate a document.
X	document-revisions	h	Measure it
X	document-revisions	code	sudo du -sh /System/Volumes/Data/.DocumentRevisions-V100
X	document-revisions	h	How to clear it
X	document-revisions	p	Delete versions you don’t need, one document at a time:
X	document-revisions	li	1. Open the document and choose File › Revert To › Browse All Versions.
X	document-revisions	li	2. Click along the timeline to a version you don’t need.
X	document-revisions	li	3. Move the pointer to the top of the screen to show the menu bar, then choose File › Revert To › Delete This Version.
X	document-revisions	p	To drop every old version of one large document at once, select it in Finder, choose File › Duplicate, delete the original, and give the copy the original’s name. The duplicate starts with no versions. Restart afterwards to give macOS a chance to tidy its store.
X	document-revisions	p	Don’t remove the folder, or anything in it, with `sudo rm`. Besides losing every version, taking pieces out leaves the database out of step with what it stores.
X	document-revisions	h	Sources
X	document-revisions	src	Apple: View and restore past versions of documents on Mac: https://support.apple.com/guide/mac-help/mh40710/mac
X	document-revisions	src	Howard Oakley, The Eclectic Light Company: Managing macOS versioning and the .DocumentRevisions-V100 folder: https://eclecticlight.co/2025/09/08/managing-macos-versioning-and-the-documentrevisions-v100-folder/
X	fseventsd	p	**No, leave it.** `.fseventsd` is macOS’s running record of which folders changed on a disk. Backup and sync tools use it to catch up on changes without rescanning everything.
X	fseventsd	p	**What deleting it costs:** the tools that rely on that history lose it, and may have to scan the whole disk to work out what changed. Apple tells developers never to work with these files directly.
X	fseventsd	p	**Does it come back?** At once: macOS’s `fseventsd` service keeps writing it as files change.
X	fseventsd	h	Measure it
X	fseventsd	code	sudo du -sh /System/Volumes/Data/.fseventsd
X	fseventsd	h	How to clear it
X	fseventsd	p	On your Mac’s own disk, leave it. Deleting it gains little and throws away the change history that backup and sync apps depend on. Apple even tells developers not to purge event history, because no app can assume it’s the only one using it.
X	fseventsd	p	On an external drive it does no harm either: other computers ignore it, and a Mac creates it again. Apple’s old developer guide describes switching logging off for a volume with an empty file named `no_log` inside `.fseventsd`, meant for cases like backup volumes. An Apple engineer has since said he wouldn’t recommend opting general-purpose volumes out, and a developer reported that it no longer seemed to work on an APFS volume in macOS 15.5.
X	fseventsd	h	Sources
X	fseventsd	src	Apple: File System Event Security (File System Events Programming Guide): https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/Darwin/Conceptual/FSEvents_ProgGuide/FileSystemEventSecurity/FileSystemEventSecurity.html
X	fseventsd	src	Apple: Using the File System Events API: https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/Darwin/Conceptual/FSEvents_ProgGuide/UsingtheFSEventsFramework/UsingtheFSEventsFramework.html
X	fseventsd	src	Apple Developer Forums: opting a volume out of file system events: https://developer.apple.com/forums/thread/785955
X	library-logs	p	**Yes, your old ones.** Log files record what apps and installers did, and once they’re a week or so old nobody reads them unless something has gone wrong. Clearing old files from your own `~/Library/Logs` is harmless.
X	library-logs	p	**What it costs you:** the history you’d want when chasing a problem. Keep recent logs, and any report a developer has asked you for.
X	library-logs	p	**Does it come back?** New logs appear as apps keep writing; the old ones are gone for good. “All logs” isn’t something you can or need to delete: macOS’s main log is managed by macOS, which trims it itself.
X	library-logs	h	Measure it
X	library-logs	code	du -sh ~/Library/Logs/* 2>/dev/null | sort -h | tail -10
X	library-logs	h	How to clear it
X	library-logs	li	1. Quit the app whose logs you’re clearing, so it isn’t writing to them.
X	library-logs	li	2. In Finder, open `~/Library/Logs` with Go › Go to Folder and move old files, or a whole app’s folder, to the Trash. Leave the `Logs` folder itself.
X	library-logs	li	3. For reports, use Console: select one under Reports and choose File › Move to Trash.
X	library-logs	li	4. Empty the Trash.
X	library-logs	p	In Terminal, first list the files in your own logs that haven’t changed for over a week, then delete exactly those:
X	library-logs	code	find ~/Library/Logs -type f -mtime +7 -print
X	library-logs	code	find ~/Library/Logs -type f -mtime +7 -delete
X	library-logs	p	Leave `/Library/Logs` and `/private/var/log` to macOS unless one file there has run away; then remove that file in Finder once you’ve dealt with whatever writes it. Don’t try to clear the unified log: Apple says that when its store passes a set size, macOS purges the oldest messages to make room.
X	library-logs	h	Sources
X	library-logs	src	Apple: View reports in Console on Mac: https://support.apple.com/guide/console/cnsl664be99a/mac
X	library-logs	src	Apple Developer: Logging (the unified logging system): https://developer.apple.com/documentation/os/logging
X	library-logs	src	Apple Developer: Generating log messages from your code (storage limits): https://developer.apple.com/documentation/os/generating-log-messages-from-your-code
X	assetsv2	p	**No.** AssetsV2 is where macOS keeps parts it downloads after it’s installed: Apple Intelligence and Siri models, voices, dictionaries, fonts, security data and software updates on their way in. System Integrity Protection guards it, and macOS fetches what it needs again.
X	assetsv2	p	**What frees space instead:** turning off features you don’t use, installing updates that are waiting, and clearing what’s yours elsewhere on the Mac.
X	assetsv2	p	**Does it come back?** Yes. macOS manages what’s in it, adding and replacing assets as features and updates need them.
X	assetsv2	h	Measure it
X	assetsv2	code	du -sh /System/Library/AssetsV2/* 2>/dev/null | sort -h | tail -10
X	assetsv2	h	How to clear it
X	assetsv2	p	You can’t delete it while macOS is running: SIP blocks it, even with `sudo`. Getting around that means starting up from Recovery or turning SIP off, and neither is worth it, since macOS can download the files again.
X	assetsv2	li	1. **Turn off what you don’t use.** On macOS 15 and 26, turning off Apple Intelligence in System Settings › Apple Intelligence & Siri removes its models. macOS 27 has no single switch: what does and doesn’t free space there.
X	assetsv2	li	2. **Install waiting updates.** If Software Update says an update has downloaded, install it rather than hunting for its files.
X	assetsv2	li	3. **Free space where you’re in charge:** old iPhone backups, macOS installers and app caches.
X	assetsv2	h	Sources
X	assetsv2	src	Apple: How to get Apple Intelligence (storage it needs): https://support.apple.com/en-us/121115
X	assetsv2	src	Howard Oakley, The Eclectic Light Company: How macOS 26 Tahoe updates, download and installation: https://eclecticlight.co/2026/03/18/how-macos-26-tahoe-updates-4-download-preparation-and-installation/
X	deleted-users	p	**Yes, once you’ve checked it.** When an account is deleted and its home folder saved in a disk image, macOS puts that image here. It’s an archive of that person’s files, and nothing on the Mac uses it.
X	deleted-users	p	**What it costs you:** possibly the only copy of that account’s documents, photos and mail. Open the image and look before you delete it.
X	deleted-users	p	**Does it come back?** No. Once the image is deleted and the Trash emptied, the account can’t be restored from it.
X	deleted-users	h	Measure it
X	deleted-users	code	sudo du -sh '/Users/Deleted Users'/*
X	deleted-users	h	How to clear it
X	deleted-users	li	1. Double-click the image. It opens like a drive and shows the old home folder.
X	deleted-users	li	2. Look through Documents, Desktop, Pictures and Downloads for anything someone still wants, and copy it to your own folders or an external drive.
X	deleted-users	li	3. If you’re not sure, keep a copy of the whole image on an external drive before going further.
X	deleted-users	li	4. Eject the image, then drag it to the Trash. Finder asks for an administrator’s name and password.
X	deleted-users	li	5. Empty the Trash to get the space back.
X	deleted-users	p	To bring the account back instead, Apple’s steps copy the home folder from the opened image into `/Users`, holding Option as you drag it, then create a new account whose short name matches the folder.
X	deleted-users	h	Sources
X	deleted-users	src	Apple: Delete a user or group on Mac: https://support.apple.com/guide/mac-help/mchlp1557/mac
X	deleted-users	src	Apple: Restore a deleted user on Mac: https://support.apple.com/guide/mac-help/mchlp2968/mac
X	containers	p	**No, not the folder, and not the container of an app you use.** Each container holds everything one sandboxed app keeps: its documents, settings, caches and databases. Deleting one is like reinstalling that app from scratch, and anything saved only inside it is lost.
X	containers	p	**The exception:** the container of an app you’ve deleted and won’t use again. Removing an app doesn’t remove its container, so that one is a leftover, once you’ve checked nothing in it is yours.
X	containers	p	**Does it come back?** As a fresh, empty container the next time the app opens; the old contents don’t.
X	containers	h	Measure it
X	containers	code	du -sh ~/Library/Containers/* 2>/dev/null | sort -h | tail -10
X	containers	h	How to clear it
X	containers	p	For an app you use, free space from inside the app rather than deleting its container: remove downloads, old projects or offline content in its settings, and for Docker, use `docker system prune` instead of touching its files.
X	containers	p	For an app you’ve removed:
X	containers	li	1. Make sure the app is really gone: not in Applications, not in `~/Applications`, and not about to be reinstalled from the App Store.
X	containers	li	2. Open its container in Finder and look in `Data/Documents` and `Data/Library/Application Support` for anything of yours, such as exports or saved files. Copy out what you want to keep.
X	containers	li	3. Move the container to the Trash; macOS may ask for permission first. Empty the Trash when you’re sure.
X	containers	p	If the app has an uninstaller or its maker publishes removal steps, follow those instead: they name the exact folders. Leave the Containers folder itself alone, and Apple’s own `com.apple.…` containers, such as Mail’s.
X	containers	h	Sources
X	containers	src	Apple: Accessing files from the macOS App Sandbox: https://developer.apple.com/documentation/security/accessing-files-from-the-macos-app-sandbox
X	containers	src	Howard Oakley, The Eclectic Light Company: What are all those Containers?: https://eclecticlight.co/2024/08/05/what-are-all-those-containers/
X	containers	src	Microsoft: Uninstall Office for Mac: https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/uninstall-office-for-mac-eefa1199-5b58-43af-8a3d-b73dc1a8cae3
X	saved-application-state	p	**Yes.** It only remembers which windows each app had open, and where, so they reappear when you reopen the app or restart the Mac. Your documents and settings aren’t in it.
X	saved-application-state	p	**What it costs you:** the next time you open an app, it starts with a fresh window instead of your last layout. Save your work first: an app that brings back unsaved windows after a restart relies on it.
X	saved-application-state	p	**Does it come back?** Yes. Apps write their saved state again as you use them, unless you turn the feature off.
X	saved-application-state	h	Measure it
X	saved-application-state	code	du -sh ~/Library/'Saved Application State'
X	saved-application-state	code	du -sh ~/Library/'Saved Application State'/* 2>/dev/null | sort -h | tail -8
X	saved-application-state	h	How to clear it
X	saved-application-state	p	For one app that misbehaves:
X	saved-application-state	li	1. Quit the app.
X	saved-application-state	li	2. Open `~/Library/Saved Application State` and move that app’s `.savedState` folder to the Trash.
X	saved-application-state	li	3. Open the app again. It starts with a clean window.
X	saved-application-state	p	For a fresh start without deleting anything, hold Shift while you open the app. Apple says the app then forgets the windows that were open when you last quit it, and no settings change.
X	saved-application-state	p	To clear every app’s saved windows in Terminal, quit your apps first, then:
X	saved-application-state	code	rm -rf ~/Library/'Saved Application State'/*
X	saved-application-state	p	To stop windows coming back at all, turn on **Close windows when quitting an application** in System Settings › Desktop & Dock, and untick **Reopen windows when logging back in** in the dialog that appears when you shut down or restart.
X	saved-application-state	h	Sources
X	saved-application-state	src	Apple: Prevent apps and windows from reopening on Mac: https://support.apple.com/en-us/102318
X	preferences-plist-files	p	**Not all of them.** Each `.plist` holds one app’s settings, and macOS keeps its own there too, for the Dock, Finder, keyboard and much more. Deleting the lot resets every app and much of macOS to defaults, for very little space.
X	preferences-plist-files	p	**What one costs you:** that app’s settings. Resetting a single app is a standard fix when it misbehaves, and the reliable way is the `defaults` command with the app quit.
X	preferences-plist-files	p	**Does it come back?** A fresh file with default settings appears as soon as the app runs again; your old choices don’t.
X	preferences-plist-files	h	Measure it
X	preferences-plist-files	code	du -sh ~/Library/Preferences
X	preferences-plist-files	code	ls -lS ~/Library/Preferences | head -10
X	preferences-plist-files	h	How to clear it
X	preferences-plist-files	li	1. Quit the app, and any helper it keeps running in the menu bar.
X	preferences-plist-files	li	2. Find its ID. It’s the file’s name without `.plist`, or Spotlight’s metadata can tell you, as in the first command below.
X	preferences-plist-files	li	3. Delete its settings with `defaults delete` and that ID. Going through macOS’s preferences service means a copy held in memory can’t write the old settings back.
X	preferences-plist-files	li	4. Open the app. It starts with default settings.
X	preferences-plist-files	code	mdls -name kMDItemCFBundleIdentifier /Applications/App.app
X	preferences-plist-files	code	defaults delete com.example.app
X	preferences-plist-files	p	In Finder, the equivalent is moving that single `.plist` to the Trash with the app quit. Don’t delete `com.apple.…` files to tidy up, and don’t empty the folder: that resets Dock layouts, Finder views, keyboard shortcuts and accessibility settings in one go.
X	preferences-plist-files	h	Sources
X	preferences-plist-files	src	Apple: macOS Library Directory Details (Preferences): https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/FileManagement/Conceptual/FileSystemProgrammingGuide/MacOSXDirectories/MacOSXDirectories.html
X	preferences-plist-files	src	Howard Oakley, The Eclectic Light Company: Settings, preferences and defaults: https://eclecticlight.co/2026/05/19/settings-preferences-and-defaults/
X	group-containers	p	**No.** Each folder here holds data that several apps from one developer share, or that an app shares with its widgets and extensions. Deleting one can wipe data more than one app depends on, from Outlook’s mail to the notes in Apple’s Notes.
X	group-containers	p	**When removing one is right:** after you’ve removed every app that shares it, ideally by following the maker’s own uninstall steps, which name the exact folders.
X	group-containers	p	**Does it come back?** As an empty folder if any app in the group runs again; what was in it doesn’t.
X	group-containers	h	Measure it
X	group-containers	code	du -sh ~/Library/'Group Containers'/* 2>/dev/null | sort -h | tail -10
X	group-containers	h	How to clear it
X	group-containers	li	1. Remove every app in the group first. For Microsoft Office that means Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Outlook and OneNote, not just one of them.
X	group-containers	li	2. Back up the folder if it might hold something you’ll want, such as mail kept only on this Mac.
X	group-containers	li	3. Follow the maker’s removal steps where they exist. Microsoft’s list `UBF8T346G9.ms`, `UBF8T346G9.Office` and `UBF8T346G9.OfficeOsfWebHost`, and warn that Outlook data is removed with them.
X	group-containers	li	4. Move the folders to the Trash in Finder, then empty it.
X	group-containers	p	Leave every `group.com.apple.…` folder alone. They belong to Apple’s own apps, many of which can’t be removed at all, so their folders are never leftovers.
X	group-containers	h	Sources
X	group-containers	src	Apple: Accessing app group containers in your existing macOS app: https://developer.apple.com/documentation/xcode/accessing-app-group-containers
X	group-containers	src	Apple: Configuring app groups: https://developer.apple.com/documentation/xcode/configuring-app-groups
X	group-containers	src	Microsoft: Uninstall Office for Mac: https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/uninstall-office-for-mac-eefa1199-5b58-43af-8a3d-b73dc1a8cae3
X	swap-files	p	**No.** Swap files hold parts of memory macOS moved to the disk to make room. They’re in use for as long as your Mac runs, and macOS creates and removes them itself as memory demand changes.
X	swap-files	p	**What to do instead:** close what’s using the memory. Quitting heavy apps brings swap use down, and after a restart the Mac starts with none in use.
X	swap-files	p	**Does it come back?** Whenever memory runs short again. A lot of swap is a sign the Mac needs more memory for what you ask of it, not a cleaning problem.
X	swap-files	h	Measure it
X	swap-files	code	sysctl vm.swapusage
X	swap-files	h	How to clear it
X	swap-files	p	The files belong to the running system, and removing one would pull memory out from under running apps. macOS offers no way to delete them, and you don’t need one:
X	swap-files	li	1. Open Activity Monitor, click Memory, and sort by the Memory column to see which apps use the most.
X	swap-files	li	2. Quit what you don’t need: browsers with many tabs, virtual machines, large editors.
X	swap-files	li	3. Restart if swap stays high. The Mac starts with fresh memory and nothing in swap.
X	swap-files	li	4. If Memory Pressure is often yellow or red, the Mac could use more memory: Apple says yellow means it might eventually need more RAM, and red that it needs more. System Information shows whether yours can be upgraded; on Macs with Apple silicon it can’t, so it’s a choice for your next Mac.
X	swap-files	h	Sources
X	swap-files	src	Apple: View memory usage in Activity Monitor on Mac: https://support.apple.com/guide/activity-monitor/view-memory-usage-actmntr1004/mac
X	swap-files	src	Apple: Check if your Mac needs more RAM: https://support.apple.com/kb/PH19650
X	swap-files	src	Apple Platform Security: Role of Apple File System (the VM volume): https://support.apple.com/guide/security/seca6147599e/web
X	system-library-caches	p	**No, leave it to macOS.** This is the cache folder shared by every account on the Mac and by background services, and much of it belongs to macOS itself. Parts of it are guarded, so you couldn’t clear everything even if you tried.
X	system-library-caches	p	**What clearing it by hand costs:** an administrator password, for little space. The supported reset for system caches is a safe-mode startup, which clears some of them and lets macOS make them again.
X	system-library-caches	p	**Does it come back?** Yes. Whatever is cleared is rebuilt as it’s needed, which is the other reason clearing it rarely pays.
X	system-library-caches	h	Measure it
X	system-library-caches	code	sudo du -sh /Library/Caches/* 2>/dev/null | sort -h | tail -10
X	system-library-caches	h	How to clear it
X	system-library-caches	p	For macOS’s own caches, use Apple’s way: start up in safe mode once, then restart normally. Apple’s support article says safe mode clears some system caches, including font caches and the kernel cache, which are created again automatically as needed.
X	system-library-caches	li	1. On a Mac with Apple silicon: shut down, then press and hold the power button until the startup options appear. Select your startup disk, hold Shift and click Continue in Safe Mode.
X	system-library-caches	li	2. On an Intel Mac: turn it on or restart, and hold Shift until the login window appears.
X	system-library-caches	li	3. Log in. “Safe Boot” shows in the menu bar of the login window. Then restart normally.
X	system-library-caches	p	For a third-party app’s folder that has grown large, use the app’s own settings or uninstaller first. If the app is already gone, move its folder from `/Library/Caches` to the Trash in Finder and enter your administrator password when asked. Leave Apple’s folders alone, and don’t use `sudo rm` here.
X	system-library-caches	h	Sources
X	system-library-caches	src	Apple: Start up your Mac in safe mode: https://support.apple.com/en-us/116946
X	system-library-caches	src	Apple Mac User Guide: Start up your Mac in safe mode: https://support.apple.com/guide/mac-help/mh21245/mac
X	var-folders	p	**No, not by hand.** It holds every account’s temporary folder and a second cache folder, both in use right now by running apps and macOS services. Deleting from it underneath them can make them fail until you restart.
X	var-folders	p	**What to do instead:** let macOS clear it. Apple documents that temporary files untouched for three days may be removed by the system, and that the cache folder is emptied when the Mac starts up in safe mode.
X	var-folders	p	**Does it come back?** All the time: everything that runs uses it, so it’s never empty, and isn’t meant to be.
X	var-folders	h	Measure it
X	var-folders	code	du -sh $TMPDIR $(getconf DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR)
X	var-folders	h	How to clear it
X	var-folders	li	1. Restart. Apps that were running let go of their temporary files, and macOS’s cleanup runs as the Mac starts up.
X	var-folders	li	2. If one app’s folder in `T` or `C` is huge, quit that app, then move just that folder, or those files, to the Trash. The name usually says which app it is.
X	var-folders	li	3. To empty the cache folder, start up in safe mode once, then restart normally.
X	var-folders	p	Never run `rm -rf` on `/private/var/folders`, with or without `sudo`. It deletes files that background services have open, along with other accounts’ files.
X	var-folders	h	Sources
X	var-folders	src	confstr(3) manual page: the per-user temporary and cache folders: https://keith.github.io/xcode-man-pages/confstr.3.html
X	var-folders	src	Apple Developer Forums: when /tmp and $TMPDIR are cleaned up: https://developer.apple.com/forums/thread/71382
X	var-folders	src	Apple Mac User Guide: Start up your Mac in safe mode: https://support.apple.com/guide/mac-help/mh21245/mac
X	dyld-cache	p	**No.** The dyld shared cache is where macOS keeps all of its system libraries, the code every app on your Mac runs on. Since macOS 11 those libraries no longer exist as separate files, so this cache is the only copy.
X	dyld-cache	p	**What deleting it would cost:** apps, and macOS itself, couldn’t start. That’s why it lives in parts of the system that are sealed and checked, where you can’t change it.
X	dyld-cache	p	**Does it come back?** It’s replaced with each macOS update and never needs cleaning by hand. There’s nothing to win here.
X	dyld-cache	h	Measure it
X	dyld-cache	code	du -sh /System/Volumes/Preboot/Cryptexes/OS/System/Library/dyld
X	dyld-cache	h	How to clear it
X	dyld-cache	p	There’s no safe way to remove it, and on current macOS no way at all with the Mac running: the system volume is sealed, and the cryptex is verified before it’s mounted. Don’t look for workarounds such as turning off System Integrity Protection.
X	dyld-cache	p	If a disk map showed it to you as something large:
X	dyld-cache	li	- Count it as part of macOS, like the rest of the system. Its size is set by Apple and changes with updates.
X	dyld-cache	li	- Get space back from what’s yours instead: System Data, app caches and old macOS installers.
X	dyld-cache	h	Sources
X	dyld-cache	src	Apple: macOS Big Sur 11.0.1 Release Notes (the built-in dynamic linker cache): https://developer.apple.com/documentation/macos-release-notes/macos-big-sur-11_0_1-release-notes
X	dyld-cache	src	Howard Oakley, The Eclectic Light Company: How Cryptexes are changing macOS Ventura: https://eclecticlight.co/2023/04/05/how-cryptexes-are-changing-macos-ventura/
X	dyld-cache	src	Howard Oakley, The Eclectic Light Company: Which cryptexes does macOS Tahoe load?: https://eclecticlight.co/2026/01/19/which-cryptexes-does-macos-tahoe-load/
X	dyld-cache	src	WebKit bug 231092: the shared cache moved from /private/var/db/dyld to /System/Library/dyld: https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=231092
X	sleepimage	p	**No, leave it.** It’s where macOS saves a copy of memory so a sleeping Mac can pick up where it was, even if the battery runs out. macOS writes it again the next time the Mac sleeps, so deleting it wins nothing for long.
X	sleepimage	p	**What losing it would cost:** protection for your open work if power fails while the Mac sleeps. The only real way to be rid of it is a power setting, and Apple’s documentation says to use caution with it.
X	sleepimage	p	**Does it come back?** Yes, by itself. On a laptop it’s part of normal sleep.
X	sleepimage	h	Measure it
X	sleepimage	code	ls -lh /private/var/vm/
X	sleepimage	code	pmset -g | grep -E 'hibernate|standby'
X	sleepimage	h	How to clear it
X	sleepimage	p	Removing the file would take `sudo rm` on a system file, and it gains nothing: macOS writes a new one the next time the Mac sleeps. Don’t.
X	sleepimage	p	If you really want no sleep image, it’s the setting that matters, not the file. Apple’s `pmset` manual says hibernation images stop only when `hibernatemode`, `standby` and `autopoweroff` are all 0, and marks the hibernation settings “Please use caution”. On a laptop, that means a battery running flat during sleep takes your open work with it, so SiftMac’s advice is to keep the defaults.
X	sleepimage	p	To free space that stays free, look at what you control: Time Machine’s local snapshots, old macOS installers and the rest of System Data.
X	sleepimage	h	Sources
X	sleepimage	src	pmset(1) manual page: hibernatemode, hibernatefile and standby: https://keith.github.io/xcode-man-pages/pmset.1.html
X	spotlight-index	p	**Not by hand.** `.Spotlight-V100` is one disk’s search index, the thing Spotlight, Finder’s search, Mail’s search and many apps look things up in. If it’s huge or broken, have Spotlight rebuild it; on an external drive you never search, turn indexing off instead.
X	spotlight-index	p	**What a rebuild costs you:** searches come up short for a while. Apple says indexing can take hours, or even days, depending on how much is on the disk.
X	spotlight-index	p	**Does it come back?** Yes: Spotlight indexes the disk again, and the index grows back to fit what’s on it.
X	spotlight-index	h	Measure it
X	spotlight-index	code	sudo du -sh /System/Volumes/Data/.Spotlight-V100
X	spotlight-index	code	mdutil -s /
X	spotlight-index	h	How to clear it
X	spotlight-index	p	Apple’s way to rebuild the index of a disk or folder:
X	spotlight-index	li	1. Open System Settings › Spotlight, or Siri & Spotlight on older versions of macOS.
X	spotlight-index	li	2. Click Search Privacy, or Spotlight Privacy.
X	spotlight-index	li	3. Add the disk or folder to the list, wait a few seconds, then select it and remove it again.
X	spotlight-index	li	4. Click Done. Spotlight indexes it again from the start.
X	spotlight-index	p	In Terminal, this erases the startup disk’s index so that Spotlight rebuilds it:
X	spotlight-index	code	sudo mdutil -E /
X	spotlight-index	p	For an external drive you never search, turn indexing off for it so the index doesn’t grow back, then remove the old one with Spotlight’s own tool rather than Finder (put the drive’s name in):
X	spotlight-index	code	sudo mdutil -i off /Volumes/DriveName
X	spotlight-index	code	sudo mdutil -X /Volumes/DriveName
X	spotlight-index	p	To keep the index smaller, add folders you never search, such as virtual machines or build folders, to Search Privacy; Spotlight then leaves them out.
X	spotlight-index	h	Sources
X	spotlight-index	src	Apple: Rebuild the Spotlight index on your Mac: https://support.apple.com/en-us/102321
X	library-caches	p	**Yes**, app by app, with each app quit. Everything in it is a copy an app can make again: images and web content it already loaded, compiled code, package archives. Your documents, settings and passwords are kept elsewhere.
X	library-caches	p	**What it costs you:** a little speed for a while. Apps open and load more slowly until they’ve rebuilt what they use, and package managers download again what they need.
X	library-caches	p	**Does it come back?** Yes. It starts refilling as soon as you use your apps, so clearing it buys space for a while rather than for good.
X	library-caches	h	Measure it
X	library-caches	code	du -sh ~/Library/Caches
X	library-caches	code	du -sh ~/Library/Caches/* 2>/dev/null | sort -h | tail -12
X	library-caches	h	How to clear it
X	library-caches	li	1. Find the big folders with the command above and work out which app each belongs to. The name usually tells you: `com.google.Chrome` is Chrome, `Homebrew` is Homebrew.
X	library-caches	li	2. Quit that app with `⌘Q`, so nothing is writing to its cache while you clear it.
X	library-caches	li	3. In Finder, open the app’s folder, select everything inside and move it to the Trash. Keep the folder itself, then empty the Trash.
X	library-caches	p	The same in Terminal for one app, with it quit (put the real folder name in):
X	library-caches	code	rm -rf ~/Library/Caches/com.example.app/*
X	library-caches	p	Where a tool has a clean command of its own, use it instead, because it knows what it can drop: `brew cleanup` for Homebrew, `pip cache purge` for pip, `yarn cache clean` for Yarn. Don’t delete `~/Library/Caches` itself, and don’t empty everything at once while apps are open.
X	library-caches	h	Sources
X	library-caches	src	Apple: macOS Library Directory Details (File System Programming Guide): https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/FileManagement/Conceptual/FileSystemProgrammingGuide/MacOSXDirectories/MacOSXDirectories.html
X	dot-underscore-files	p	**Yes**, on a drive that goes into a TV, car stereo, camera or Windows PC. A `._` file holds only Mac extras for the file of the same name, such as Finder tags and the quarantine flag, never the file’s contents.
X	dot-underscore-files	p	**What it costs you:** those extras, for the files whose `._` partner you remove. For music, photos and videos that’s nothing you’d miss; very old Mac files that keep data in a resource fork can stop working.
X	dot-underscore-files	p	**Does it come back?** Yes, whenever a Mac copies or changes files on that drive again. `dot_clean`, built into macOS, clears them.
X	dot-underscore-files	h	Measure it
X	dot-underscore-files	code	find /Volumes/MyDrive -name '._*' -type f | wc -l
X	dot-underscore-files	code	find /Volumes/MyDrive -name '._*' -type f -exec du -k {} + | awk '{s+=$1} END {print s " KB"}'
X	dot-underscore-files	h	How to clear it
X	dot-underscore-files	p	`dot_clean` comes with macOS. With the drive connected:
X	dot-underscore-files	li	1. Find the drive’s name in Finder’s sidebar. In Terminal it’s `/Volumes/` followed by that name.
X	dot-underscore-files	li	2. To remove every `._` file on it, run `dot_clean -m` on the drive. Its manual says `-m` always deletes dot-underscore files.
X	dot-underscore-files	li	3. To remove only orphans, the `._` files whose real file was deleted on a TV or a PC, use `-n` instead: it deletes a dot-underscore file only when there’s no matching file.
X	dot-underscore-files	li	4. Eject the drive in Finder before unplugging it.
X	dot-underscore-files	code	dot_clean -m /Volumes/MyDrive
X	dot-underscore-files	p	Run with no option, `dot_clean` merges each `._` file’s information into its partner file; `-m` also deletes the `._` files, which is what a drive for other devices needs. A zip file’s `__MACOSX` folder can simply go to the Trash after unzipping.
X	dot-underscore-files	h	Sources
X	dot-underscore-files	src	dot_clean(1) man page: merge ._* files with corresponding native files: https://keith.github.io/xcode-man-pages/dot_clean.1.html
X	dot-underscore-files	src	Apple Developer Forums: Extended Attributes and Zip Archives (Apple DTS on AppleDouble): https://developer.apple.com/forums/thread/690457
X	dot-underscore-files	src	Wikipedia: AppleSingle and AppleDouble formats: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AppleSingle_and_AppleDouble_formats
X	ds-store-files	p	**Yes.** A `.DS_Store` file only remembers how Finder shows its folder: the view, where icons sit and similar settings. None of your files are inside it.
X	ds-store-files	p	**What it costs you:** that folder opens with Finder’s default view until you arrange it again. Each file is a few kilobytes, so there’s no real space to win.
X	ds-store-files	p	**Does it come back?** Yes. Finder writes a new one when it opens the folder again, so clearing them is mainly for folders you share.
X	ds-store-files	h	Measure it
X	ds-store-files	code	find ~ -name .DS_Store -type f 2>/dev/null | wc -l
X	ds-store-files	code	find ~ -name .DS_Store -type f -exec du -k {} + 2>/dev/null | awk '{s+=$1} END {print s " KB"}'
X	ds-store-files	h	How to clear it
X	ds-store-files	p	On your own Mac there’s no reason to hunt them down. Clear them from a folder you’re about to share, a drive that goes into another device, or a code repository:
X	ds-store-files	li	1. **One folder and everything in it:** in Terminal, run the command below with your folder’s path in place of the example. It removes only files named exactly `.DS_Store`.
X	ds-store-files	li	2. **A USB drive or SD card:** close any Finder window showing it, run the same command on `/Volumes/` followed by the drive’s name, then eject it.
X	ds-store-files	li	3. **A Git repository:** add `.DS_Store` to Git’s global ignore file so it’s never committed again, and remove any already committed with `git rm --cached .DS_Store`.
X	ds-store-files	code	find /Volumes/MyDrive -name .DS_Store -type f -delete
X	ds-store-files	code	mkdir -p ~/.config/git && echo .DS_Store >> ~/.config/git/ignore
X	ds-store-files	p	Git uses `~/.config/git/ignore` as its global ignore file by default, unless `core.excludesFile` or `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` points somewhere else. For network shares, Apple documents a setting that stops Finder using `.DS_Store` files on SMB shares; log out and back in after running it:
X	ds-store-files	code	defaults write com.apple.desktopservices DSDontWriteNetworkStores -bool TRUE
X	ds-store-files	p	Apple presents it as a way to make browsing a share faster; folders there then list in plain alphanumeric order. Run it again with `FALSE` at the end to undo it.
X	ds-store-files	h	Sources
X	ds-store-files	src	Apple: Adjust SMB browsing behavior in macOS: https://support.apple.com/en-us/102064
X	ds-store-files	src	Wikipedia: .DS_Store: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/.DS_Store
X	ds-store-files	src	Git documentation: gitignore (the global ignore file): https://git-scm.com/docs/gitignore
X	font-caches	p	**Yes**, with Apple’s tools. Font caches are databases macOS builds from the fonts you have installed, so resetting them deletes no fonts. It’s a repair for fonts that misbehave, not a way to free space.
X	font-caches	p	**What it costs you:** a restart or a log out, and some Font Book choices to redo: fonts you turned off can be back on, and fonts you activated from other folders may need activating again.
X	font-caches	p	**Does it come back?** Yes. macOS builds new databases from the fonts in the standard font folders after you log out or restart.
X	font-caches	h	Measure it
X	font-caches	code	du -sh "$(getconf DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR)com.apple.FontRegistry"
X	font-caches	h	How to clear it
X	font-caches	p	Start with the gentlest step that fixes the problem:
X	font-caches	li	1. **Check the fonts first.** In Font Book, select fonts and choose File › Validate Selection. Fonts that fail show in red, and you can remove them with Remove Checked. File › Resolve Duplicates deals with two copies of the same font.
X	font-caches	li	2. **Start up in safe mode.** Apple says safe mode clears some system caches, including font caches, which macOS creates again as needed. Start up in safe mode, then restart normally and look at the fonts again.
X	font-caches	li	3. **Reset your own font caches from Terminal.** Quit your apps, run `atsutil databases -removeUser`, then log out and back in, or restart.
X	font-caches	code	atsutil databases -removeUser
X	font-caches	p	Its manual says `-removeUser` removes the font databases for the active user only, which is enough for a problem in your own account; `-remove` clears the system’s databases as well. Older guides also tell you to run `atsutil server -shutdown` and `atsutil server -ping`. The `atsutil` in recent macOS has no `server` command (`atsutil help` lists what yours has); logging out or restarting does that job.
X	font-caches	h	Sources
X	font-caches	src	Apple: Start up your Mac in safe mode: https://support.apple.com/en-us/116946
X	font-caches	src	atsutil(8) man page: font registration system utility: https://keith.github.io/xcode-man-pages/atsutil.8.html
X	font-caches	src	Apple: Install and validate fonts in Font Book on Mac: https://support.apple.com/guide/font-book/fntbk1000/mac
X	iphone-software-updates	p	**Yes.** An `.ipsw` file is a copy of iOS or iPadOS your Mac downloaded to update or restore a device. Once that’s done nothing needs it, and your iPhone backups aren’t in it.
X	iphone-software-updates	p	**What it costs you:** a fresh download the next time you update or restore a device from this Mac.
X	iphone-software-updates	p	**Does it come back?** Yes, when it’s needed: the Mac downloads the software again for the next update or restore.
X	iphone-software-updates	h	Measure it
X	iphone-software-updates	code	du -sh ~/Library/iTunes/*/*.ipsw 2>/dev/null
X	iphone-software-updates	h	How to clear it
X	iphone-software-updates	li	1. Make sure no update or restore is running; if a device is connected, let Finder finish.
X	iphone-software-updates	li	2. Open System Settings › General › Storage, click ⓘ next to iOS Files, select the software files (not the backups) and click Delete.
X	iphone-software-updates	li	3. Or open the folder in Finder, drag the `.ipsw` files to the Trash and empty it.
X	iphone-software-updates	p	Leave the backups in `~/Library/Application Support/MobileSync/Backup` alone unless you mean to delete one: that’s a separate choice, with no undo.
X	iphone-software-updates	h	Sources
X	iphone-software-updates	src	Apple: Change Storage settings on Mac (iOS Files): https://support.apple.com/guide/mac-help/change-storage-settings-on-mac-mchl3d437fbc/mac
