X	iphone-software-updates	src	Apple: If you can't update or restore your iPhone or iPod touch: https://support.apple.com/118106
X	keychains	p	**No.** `~/Library/Keychains` holds your login keychain and this Mac’s copy of your iCloud Keychain or Local Items: saved passwords, passkeys, certificates and private keys. Deleting it frees almost no space and loses whatever isn’t stored anywhere else.
X	keychains	p	**What it costs you:** apps and websites ask you to sign in again, and certificates and keys that lived only on this Mac are gone.
X	keychains	p	**Does it come back?** Not by itself. Passwords you sync with iCloud Keychain are still in iCloud, but anything kept only on this Mac needs a backup to come back.
X	keychains	h	Measure it
X	keychains	code	du -sh ~/Library/Keychains
X	keychains	h	How to clear it
X	keychains	p	There’s no safe way to delete the folder. What people usually want is one of these, and each has its own tool:
X	keychains	li	- **Fewer saved passwords:** delete the ones you don’t use in the Passwords app, or select them in Keychain Access and delete them there. With iCloud Keychain on, the change reaches your other devices too.
X	keychains	li	- **Your Mac keeps asking for the login keychain password:** the keychain password no longer matches your login password, usually after the login password was reset. Apple recommends changing your password in Users & Groups settings so the keychain follows. Only if you don’t remember the old password, reset the default keychain: Keychain Access › Settings › Reset Default Keychains, then log out and back in. Apple warns that this deletes all the passwords saved in the keychain, and recommends doing it only when Apple Support advises it.
X	keychains	li	- **A keychain you made yourself, or one whose password you’ve forgotten:** in Keychain Access, select it in the Keychains list, choose File › Delete Keychain, and click Delete References.
X	keychains	p	Don’t delete `login.keychain-db` or the long-ID folder in Finder to start fresh. You lose every item in them, and macOS has no way to know that’s what you meant.
X	keychains	h	Sources
X	keychains	src	Apple: If you need to update your keychain password on Mac: https://support.apple.com/guide/keychain-access/kyca2429/mac
X	keychains	src	Apple: Delete a keychain in Keychain Access on Mac: https://support.apple.com/guide/keychain-access/kyca2423/mac
X	keychains	src	Apple: What is Keychain Access on Mac?: https://support.apple.com/guide/keychain-access/kyca1083/mac
X	keychains	src	Eclectic Light: Explainer: Keychains (October 2026): https://eclecticlight.co/2026/10/07/explainer-keychains/
X	macos-installers	p	**Yes, the installers.** An app named “Install macOS …” in Applications is a full macOS installer that was downloaded and never removed. It isn’t the macOS your Mac runs, and Apple lets you download it again whenever you need it.
X	macos-installers	p	**What it costs you:** nothing, unless you meant to upgrade with it soon or make a bootable USB installer from it. Keep it until you have.
X	macos-installers	p	**Does it come back?** Only if you download it again, from Software Update, the App Store or Apple’s download page.
X	macos-installers	h	How to clear it
X	macos-installers	li	1. Check which macOS you run: Apple menu › About This Mac.
X	macos-installers	li	2. An installer for that version or an older one has nothing left to do. Drag it to the Trash and empty the Trash. If Finder asks for an administrator’s name and password, an administrator account installed it.
X	macos-installers	li	3. An installer for a newer macOS you haven’t installed yet: keep it until you upgrade, or delete it and let Software Update download what it needs when you’re ready.
X	macos-installers	li	4. Want a bootable USB installer? Make it first with Apple’s `createinstallmedia` steps; Apple’s guide then says you can delete the installer from your Applications folder.
X	macos-installers	p	If the installer is open, quit it first. Never try to free space from the macOS part of your disk or anything under `/System`: that’s the system itself, and macOS protects it.
X	macos-installers	h	Sources
X	macos-installers	src	Apple: How to download and install macOS: https://support.apple.com/en-us/102662
X	macos-installers	src	Apple: Create a bootable installer for macOS: https://support.apple.com/en-us/101578
X	macos-installers	src	Apple Platform Security: Signed system volume security: https://support.apple.com/guide/security/secd698747c9/web
