X	quicklook-thumbnail-cache	p	**Yes.** It holds the little previews Finder shows instead of plain icons for photos, PDFs, videos and documents. Resetting it with Apple’s `qlmanage -r cache` loses nothing you made.
X	quicklook-thumbnail-cache	p	**What it costs you:** a moment of generic icons the next time you open a folder full of pictures, while the previews are drawn again.
X	quicklook-thumbnail-cache	p	**Does it come back?** Yes, by itself. Quick Look makes new thumbnails as you browse, so the cache fills up again.
X	quicklook-thumbnail-cache	h	Measure it
X	quicklook-thumbnail-cache	code	find "$(getconf DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR)" -maxdepth 2 -name com.apple.QuickLook.thumbnailcache -exec du -sh {} + 2>/dev/null
X	quicklook-thumbnail-cache	h	How to clear it
X	quicklook-thumbnail-cache	li	1. Open Terminal (in Applications › Utilities).
X	quicklook-thumbnail-cache	li	2. Run `qlmanage -r cache`. It resets the thumbnail cache for your account, with no password to enter and no restart needed.
X	quicklook-thumbnail-cache	li	3. If previews of one kind of file look broken as well, also run `qlmanage -r`, which reloads Quick Look’s list of generators.
X	quicklook-thumbnail-cache	code	qlmanage -r cache
X	quicklook-thumbnail-cache	p	Don’t go into `/private/var/folders` and delete things by hand. Besides this cache, that folder holds other apps’ temporary files and caches that are in use right now.
X	quicklook-thumbnail-cache	h	Sources
