X	downloads-folder	p	**Yes, whatever you no longer need.** Downloads is your folder: macOS and your apps don’t rely on what’s in it. Installers for apps you’ve already installed and zip files you’ve already unpacked are the easy wins.
X	downloads-folder	p	**What it costs you:** anything that turns out to be your only copy, like a ticket, a bank statement or a file someone sent you. Open those before they go.
X	downloads-folder	p	**Does it come back?** Not by itself. A deleted download returns only if you download it again, and not every link works twice.
X	downloads-folder	h	Measure it
X	downloads-folder	code	du -sh ~/Downloads
X	downloads-folder	code	du -sh ~/Downloads/* 2>/dev/null | sort -h | tail -20
X	downloads-folder	h	How to clear it
X	downloads-folder	li	1. In Finder, open Downloads, choose View › as List and click the Size column to put the biggest files first.
X	downloads-folder	li	2. Installers (`.dmg`, `.pkg`) for apps that are already in Applications can go. The maker’s site has a newer copy if you ever need one.
X	downloads-folder	li	3. A `.zip` next to a folder with the same name has been unpacked. Keep the folder and delete the zip, or the other way round.
X	downloads-folder	li	4. Open anything you can’t place before deleting it: boarding passes, invoices, scanned letters and contracts end up here too.
X	downloads-folder	li	5. Move what you want to keep to Documents or another folder. Select the rest, press ⌘⌫ to move it to the Trash, then empty the Trash.
X	downloads-folder	p	Delete the files, not the Downloads folder itself: browsers and apps expect it to be there.
