X	trash	p	**Yes, once you’ve looked through it.** Everything in the Trash is something you already chose to delete, and emptying it is what actually gives the space back.
X	trash	p	**What it costs you:** your last easy way back. Until then, File › Put Back returns an item to where it was; afterwards, Apple says, emptied items are permanently deleted.
X	trash	p	**Does it come back?** No. Only a backup made before can bring an emptied item back. The Trash itself fills up again as you delete things.
X	trash	h	Measure it
X	trash	code	du -sh ~/.Trash
X	trash	h	How to clear it
X	trash	li	1. Open the Trash from the Dock, switch to list view and click the Size column to see the biggest items.
X	trash	li	2. Drag out anything you want to keep, or select it and choose File › Put Back.
X	trash	li	3. Connect any external drive you deleted files from, so its Trash empties too.
X	trash	li	4. Click **Empty** in the top-right corner of the window, then Empty Trash.
X	trash	p	To delete just one item for good and keep the rest, Control-click it and choose Delete Immediately.
X	trash	p	To have macOS empty it for you, choose Finder › Settings › Advanced and select “Remove items from the Trash after 30 days”; Storage settings offers the same as Empty Trash Automatically. Items you delete from iCloud Drive leave the Trash after 30 days whatever you choose.
X	trash	h	Sources
