X	git-folder	p	**Usually not.** `.git` is the repository itself: every commit, branch, tag and stash of the project. Your current files stay if you delete it, but the folder stops being a Git repository.
X	git-folder	p	**What it costs you:** anything that exists only on this Mac: commits you haven’t pushed, local branches, stashes, and the whole history of a project that never had a remote. If everything is pushed, cloning again gets it all back.
X	git-folder	p	**Does it come back?** Only by cloning from a remote, and only what you pushed. To get space back, make the repository smaller with Git’s own tools instead.
X	git-folder	h	Measure it
X	git-folder	code	git count-objects -vH
X	git-folder	h	How to clear it
X	git-folder	p	**To make it smaller, keep it and tidy it.** Git already runs `git gc` automatically when a repository has grown a lot, but you can run it yourself, for example after deleting many branches:
X	git-folder	code	git gc
X	git-folder	p	It compresses file revisions and removes unreachable objects older than a grace period, two weeks by default. Git’s documentation says `--aggressive` isn’t worth it for most repositories, and warns that `--prune=now` can corrupt a repository if another Git process is writing to it at the same time. In projects using Git LFS, `git lfs prune --dry-run` shows which old local copies it would delete; files referenced by commits you haven’t pushed are kept.
X	git-folder	p	**To drop the history of a project you’re done with**, first make sure nothing exists only here:
X	git-folder	code	git status
X	git-folder	code	git stash list
X	git-folder	code	git log --branches --not --remotes --oneline
X	git-folder	p	The last command lists commits on local branches that no remote has. If all three come back empty, deleting the project and cloning it again later loses nothing you’ve pushed. To keep the files but drop the history, quit editors using the project and move `.git` to the Trash, which lets you change your mind until you empty it.
X	git-folder	h	Sources
X	git-folder	src	Git: git-gc documentation: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-gc
X	git-folder	src	Git: git-count-objects documentation: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-count-objects
X	git-folder	src	Git LFS: git-lfs-prune: https://github.com/git-lfs/git-lfs/blob/main/docs/man/git-lfs-prune.adoc
X	android-emulators	p	**Yes, the ones you don’t use**, from Android Studio’s Device Manager. Each emulator, or AVD, keeps its own disk images: the apps you installed, their data, an emulated SD card and saved snapshots.
X	android-emulators	p	**What it costs you:** everything on that virtual device. The Android system image it was made from stays in the SDK, so you can create a fresh emulator without downloading Android again.
X	android-emulators	p	**Does it come back?** Only as a new, empty device you create again. Its apps, sign-ins and snapshots don’t come back.
