X	pycache	p	**Yes.** Every file in `__pycache__` is compiled from a `.py` file beside it, and Python compiles it again by itself. Nothing in there is the only copy of your code.
X	pycache	p	**What it costs you:** the next import of each module is a little slower while Python compiles it again. Code doesn’t run slower once it’s loaded.
X	pycache	p	**Does it come back?** Yes, as soon as the code runs again, unless you tell Python not to write these files.
X	pycache	h	How to clear it
X	pycache	p	From the top of a project, this removes every `__pycache__` folder under it and nothing else:
X	pycache	code	cd ~/Projects/my-app
X	pycache	code	find . -type d -name __pycache__ -prune -exec rm -rf {} +
X	pycache	p	Run it inside the project folder, never from your home folder or `/`, and run the `find` part on its own first if you want to see the list. Python programs already running carry on; their modules are loaded.
X	pycache	h3	Stopping them from appearing
X	pycache	li	- `python -B`, or `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1` in your environment, tells Python not to write `.pyc` files on import. Imports then compile every time.
X	pycache	li	- `PYTHONPYCACHEPREFIX`, in Python 3.8 and later, keeps the files but writes them to a mirror tree in one folder instead of `__pycache__` folders in your code.
X	pycache	code	export PYTHONPYCACHEPREFIX="$HOME/Library/Caches/pycache"
X	pycache	h	Sources
X	pycache	src	Python tutorial: compiled Python files: https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/modules.html#compiled-python-files
X	pycache	src	Python docs: command line and environment (-B, PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE, PYTHONPYCACHEPREFIX): https://docs.python.org/3/using/cmdline.html
