X	conda-packages	h	How to clear it
X	conda-packages	li	1. Close Jupyter, editors and terminals that are using a conda environment.
X	conda-packages	li	2. Preview, then clean. `conda clean --all` removes the index cache, unused cached packages, tarballs, temporary files and log files.
X	conda-packages	li	3. Leave out `--force-pkgs-dirs` (`-f`). It isn’t part of `--all`: it removes every writable package cache, and conda’s docs warn it breaks environments installed with symlinks back to that cache.
X	conda-packages	code	conda clean --all --dry-run
X	conda-packages	code	conda clean --all
X	conda-packages	p	conda only checks environments it knows about. Ones that `conda info --envs` doesn’t list aren’t checked for links back to the cache, so make sure yours show up before cleaning.
X	conda-packages	h3	Old environments
X	conda-packages	p	Environments are yours: conda can’t rebuild one unless you saved what was in it. For each one you might want again, export it, then remove it:
X	conda-packages	code	conda env export --name old-env --from-history > old-env.yml
X	conda-packages	code	conda remove --name old-env --all
X	conda-packages	p	`--from-history` saves only the packages you asked for, which makes a file that works across platforms. To bring it back later, conda’s docs recommend `conda create --file old-env.yml`; the older `conda env create --file old-env.yml` still works. After removing environments, run `conda clean --all` again, since packages only they used are now unused.
