X	homebrew-cache	p	**Yes.** It holds bottles and installers Homebrew already downloaded; what you’ve installed runs from elsewhere. Let `brew cleanup` do it, because it also removes old versions of formulae you’ve upgraded.
X	homebrew-cache	p	**What it costs you:** reinstalling a formula or cask later downloads it again.
X	homebrew-cache	p	**Does it come back?** Yes. Every install and upgrade adds downloads, and Homebrew trims them on its own schedule.
X	homebrew-cache	h	Measure it
X	homebrew-cache	code	du -sh "$(brew --cache)"
X	homebrew-cache	code	brew cleanup --dry-run
X	homebrew-cache	h	How to clear it
X	homebrew-cache	li	1. Let any `brew install` or `brew upgrade` finish.
X	homebrew-cache	li	2. Run `brew cleanup`. It removes stale lock files and outdated downloads for every formula and cask, removes downloads more than 120 days old, and removes old versions of installed formulae.
X	homebrew-cache	li	3. To empty the download cache completely, run `brew cleanup --prune=all`, which removes cached files whatever their age.
X	homebrew-cache	li	4. Then check `brew autoremove --dry-run`. Without `--dry-run` it uninstalls formulae that were only installed as dependencies and that nothing needs now.
X	homebrew-cache	code	brew cleanup
