X	utm-virtual-machines	src	UTM Docs: Drive settings (QEMU): https://docs.getutm.app/settings-qemu/drive/drive/
X	virtualbox-virtual-machines	p	**Yes, VM by VM, from VirtualBox.** Each folder in `~/VirtualBox VMs` is one virtual machine: its settings file, its virtual disks and its snapshots. Removing a VM with its files deletes the guest system and everything saved in it.
X	virtualbox-virtual-machines	p	**What it costs you:** the whole guest. Deleting folders in Finder instead leaves VirtualBox listing a machine whose files are gone, so remove VMs in VirtualBox Manager or with `VBoxManage`.
X	virtualbox-virtual-machines	p	**Does it come back?** No. Copy or export a VM first if you might need it again.
X	virtualbox-virtual-machines	h	Measure it
X	virtualbox-virtual-machines	code	du -sh ~/VirtualBox\ VMs/* | sort -h
X	virtualbox-virtual-machines	code	VBoxManage list vms
X	virtualbox-virtual-machines	h	How to clear it
X	virtualbox-virtual-machines	li	1. Shut the VM down: VirtualBox disables Remove while a VM is running.
X	virtualbox-virtual-machines	li	2. In VirtualBox Manager, click Machines, then Control-click the VM in the machine list and choose Remove.
X	virtualbox-virtual-machines	li	3. In the dialog, choose to remove the VM’s files as well, not just its entry in the list.
X	virtualbox-virtual-machines	p	From Terminal, `VBoxManage` does the same in one step:
X	virtualbox-virtual-machines	code	VBoxManage unregistervm "<VM name>" --delete
X	virtualbox-virtual-machines	p	With `--delete`, VirtualBox deletes the VM’s hard disk images, differencing images included, its saved state files, one for each snapshot, its settings file and backups, its logs, and its folder once it’s empty. `--delete-all` also deletes DVD and floppy images in the VM’s folder that are attached only to this VM.
X	virtualbox-virtual-machines	p	**To keep a VM but shrink its disk**, first delete snapshots you no longer need in the VM’s Snapshots tab; Oracle notes this can take a long time and needs temporary disk space while it runs. Then zero out the free space inside the guest, shut the VM down and compact the disk:
X	virtualbox-virtual-machines	code	VBoxManage modifymedium disk "<path to disk>.vdi" --compact
X	virtualbox-virtual-machines	p	Compacting removes blocks that contain only zeroes, so it only frees what the guest has zeroed, with tools Oracle names such as `sdelete -z` in Windows or `zerofree` for Linux ext2 and ext3 file systems. It works on VDI images only.
X	virtualbox-virtual-machines	h	Sources
X	virtualbox-virtual-machines	src	VirtualBox manual: Removing and moving VMs, snapshots: https://www.virtualbox.org/manual/topics/working-with-vms.html
X	virtualbox-virtual-machines	src	VirtualBox manual: VBoxManage (unregistervm, modifymedium): https://www.virtualbox.org/manual/topics/vboxmanage.html
X	virtualbox-virtual-machines	src	VirtualBox manual: Where VirtualBox stores its files: https://www.virtualbox.org/manual/topics/Troubleshooting.html
