 delete snapshots you no longer need in the VM’s Snapshots tab; Oracle notes this can take a long time and needs temporary disk space while it runs. Then zero out the free space inside the guest, shut the VM down and compact the disk:\n\n```\nVBoxManage modifymedium disk \"<path to disk>.vdi\" --compact\n```\n\nCompacting removes blocks that contain only zeroes, so it only frees what the guest has zeroed, with tools Oracle names such as `sdelete -z` in Windows or `zerofree` for Linux ext2 and ext3 file systems. It works on VDI images only.","clear_commands":["VBoxManage unregistervm \"<VM name>\" --delete","VBoxManage modifymedium disk \"<path to disk>.vdi\" --compact"],"sources":[{"title":"VirtualBox manual: Removing and moving VMs, snapshots","url":"https://www.virtualbox.org/manual/topics/working-with-vms.html"},{"title":"VirtualBox manual: VBoxManage (unregistervm, modifymedium)","url":"https://www.virtualbox.org/manual/topics/vboxmanage.html"},{"title":"VirtualBox manual: Where VirtualBox stores its files","url":"https://www.virtualbox.org/manual/topics/Troubleshooting.html"}],"see_also":["parallels-virtual-machines","utm-virtual-machines","docker-raw"],"match":[{"glob":"~/VirtualBox VMs"},{"glob":"~/Library/VirtualBox"}],"url":"https://siftmac.com/safe-to-delete/virtualbox-virtual-machines","markdown_url":"https://siftmac.com/safe-to-delete/virtualbox-virtual-machines.md","published":"2026-11-21","updated":"2026-11-21"}
